{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"ebdeebaecac880d7fce7183585e7386afcaf3d104b4734049bdde138d6870c04","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_163247261910e47f38d5cec01a9cb561cd66b0df12106d6001bd304f0d33c990","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a coherent and theoretically grounded analysis of the expressive power of deep neural networks, primarily focusing on the role of depth. By introducing trajectory length as a unifying metric, the work provides a mechanistic explanation for the exponential dependence of expressivity measures on network depth. The combination of theoretical bounds, simulations on random networks, and empirical validation on MNIST and CIFAR-10 offers a comprehensive view of how architectural properties influence functional richness. The insights regarding the disproportionate influence of early layers and the trade-off between stability and expressivity during training are well-supported by the provided evidence.","paper_id":"B1TTpYKgx","paper_identity_sha256":"15dd9ea6452ec63ce8b940c8693095205f83feff35112cff08e076c5184bd3be","prompt_identity_sha256":"037f72d34153a34a4f5e545e8fb072e992b4904d3a1d58efcf452cb87b50b3ba","raw_response_sha256":"0e36eb9923f6d9ce4cd37104004255874e58d3558c4060d381a4ea5fde5f2ca9","request_identity_sha256":"a544a1c0450a96905edd9f0a494d3217a324889292c4974ca2590e1666316a23","reviewer_identity_sha256":"c1cd7c7fdd4a2cbeb45c2f21af982ad0f30bffae8ae148e3eba36479685cf360","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"22a4924da1c2f5c40ef296ee9f996ea15acd88cb097cbc207fb9bd0920e5a163","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-005","SRC-006"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a fundamentally new problem or task but rather refines the existing problem of understanding neural network expressivity. It addresses a specific gap in prior work, which the authors argue relies on unrealistic architectural assumptions or hardcoded weight comparisons. By shifting the focus to the behavior of networks after random initialization, the paper proposes a more practical and generalizable framework for studying expressivity. This reframing is presented as a meaningful contribution that allows for the derivation of characteristic properties of typical networks arising from a particular architecture, rather than just specific instances.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-013","SRC-014"],"review_text":"The paper introduces a novel analytical approach by identifying trajectory length as a fourth quantity that underlies the exponential depth dependence of other expressivity measures. The authors provide a theoretical bound (Theorem 1) on the growth of trajectory length in random networks, which is a new algorithmic or mathematical tool for analyzing network properties. This is not a minor tweak of existing methods but a new perspective that connects architectural parameters (depth, width, weight variance) to functional richness through a specific geometric quantity.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-008","SRC-016","SRC-019","SRC-022"],"review_text":"The paper provides significant novel insights by proving that three distinct measures of expressivity (transitions, activation patterns, and dichotomies) are directly proportional to trajectory length. This unification explains the observed exponential sensitivity to depth. Additionally, the paper offers a new analysis of trained networks, suggesting that the influence of a layer on expressivity is determined by the remaining depth after that layer. This insight, supported by experiments on MNIST and CIFAR-10, deepens the understanding of how training affects the input-output map, revealing a trade-off between stability and expressivity.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-009","SRC-021","SRC-023","SRC-024"],"review_text":"The experimental setup is novel in its use of trajectory length and dichotomies as metrics for evaluating expressivity in both random and trained networks. The paper introduces specific experimental protocols, such as sweeping weights along a one-dimensional trajectory to measure dichotomies and training individual layers to assess the impact of remaining depth. These setups are not merely derivative of existing benchmarks but are designed to test the specific theoretical predictions made in the paper, providing a new way to evaluate the functional richness of neural networks.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-006","SRC-008","SRC-022"],"review_text":"The paper creatively combines existing concepts from hyperplane arrangements, statistical learning theory (dichotomies), and random matrix theory to form a coherent framework for analyzing expressivity. The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: by linking local measures (transitions) to global measures (activation patterns) and function class heterogeneity (dichotomies) through trajectory length, the authors create a unified theory that explains the depth dependence of expressivity. This synergy yields new capabilities in predicting how architectural changes affect network performance.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-028"],"review_text":"The paper provides a critical assessment of prior work on expressivity, noting that much of it relies on unrealistic assumptions such as exponentially wide layers or hardcoded weights. This critique is used to motivate the paper's focus on random initialization and typical network properties. Additionally, the paper presents a negative result in the sense that it shows training can either stabilize or increase expressivity depending on the initialization, challenging the assumption that training always leads to a more expressive network. This nuanced view of the training process is a valuable contribution to the field.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"731d5d40c52f4d5bbfc82c92f0b97169c514ca608b2cef633f9799b7042698ea","paper_id":"B1TTpYKgx","paper_ordinal":11,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"0e36eb9923f6d9ce4cd37104004255874e58d3558c4060d381a4ea5fde5f2ca9","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"B1TTpYKgx","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"d441b596ee3895289c335964ecfc01b79fa269cc7c8ce1442b8874962e001372","request_identity_sha256":"a544a1c0450a96905edd9f0a494d3217a324889292c4974ca2590e1666316a23","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v5","usage":{"completion_tokens":1192,"prompt_tokens":16726,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":17918},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"a544a1c0450a96905edd9f0a494d3217a324889292c4974ca2590e1666316a23","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}